Yes — that is the right general data structure, but I would make one important change to the years.

Your proposed design:

Pre-ESSA: 2010–2014
Post-ESSA: 2018–2022

is conceptually reasonable, but NAEP isn't administered every year in every subject/grade. So you should not expect observations for every year in that range.

For the writing sample, I'd build the dataset around all available NAEP assessment cycles in your desired window rather than forcing annual observations.

NAEP

Collect:

Dimension	Values
Geography	State
Grade	4th, 8th
Subject	Math, Reading
Period	Pre/post ESSA
Subgroup	All students + disadvantaged subgroups
Outcome	Scale score + achievement levels/proficiency
Year	Every available assessment cycle

Your primary subgroup dimensions should remain:

Black
White
Hispanic
Economically disadvantaged
English learners
Students with disabilities
All students

And, as we discussed, gender can be secondary, rather than part of your core analysis.

One important timing issue

I would not define 2018 as simply "post-ESSA."

ESSA was signed in December 2015, but implementation occurred over subsequent years. So your empirical design needs to distinguish:

2010–2015: NCLB/pre-ESSA period
2016–2017: transition period
2018 onward: ESSA implementation period

That distinction is especially important because you're trying to study implementation, not merely the date Congress enacted the law.

And I would probably include 2015 in the pre-period rather than treating it as post simply because ESSA was signed that year.

